{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRf54ag8f9Y7fhQNMXuQd1","type":"tool_result","content":"A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the co\n\n|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\n| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | pod logs, committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n\n**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ar\n\n**The agent's contract is narrow.** It may edit files in its worktree and must write `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` with a `status`. Under `.ard/<issue#>/` it may change only its own role file and what its role's allowlist grants (see the `.ard/` contract). Exit 0 without that file counts as a failure, not a success, and so does a malformed file. The file counts only if this job wrote it: before the agent starts, the executor removes the job's role file from the worktree (`executor.Job.Scrub`), so a copy left from an earlier stage or run is never trusted. A role file the agent never wrote again fails the job (`role file not written by this job`, or `role file missing` when none was ever there). One it wrote again with identical bytes still counts, but only because the executor recorded the scrub in the handle (`Handle.Scrubbed`); close-out refuses an unscrubbed, unchanged role file (`\n\n**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop a\n\n**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-p\n\n**The Reaper runs every minute and decides failure, not the agent.** It marks a job failed when the process or pod is gone without a close-out, the deadline passes (it kills the job first), the heartbeat goes quiet for 10 minutes, or the job is `:running` with no record in local state. Every failure goes through close-out, so it is resumable and never double-comments:\n\n1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\n2. **Close-out.** Commit the partial output to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` (on `main` or the branch, as a normal close-out would), post one comment with the reason, the last 50 redacted log lines and the time the evidence is kept until, then remove `ard:<stage>:<role>:running` and add `ard:executor:failed`. A transient cause instead hands off to the automatic retry, and a usage limit pauses the issue until the reset (see Retries are explicit). A log that cannot be committed (a worker that stopped before its first push has no branch) never turns a retry into a failure.\n3. **Retention.** The worktree and job home are kept 24 hours after the job ended, then removed, together with the host logs of a failed job (the raw combined log is already deleted at close-out once its redacted copy is committed). A human extends this by adding `ard:keep` to the issue (or PR, for a review): the Reaper looks again 24 hours later. If it cannot read the issue it keeps everything. Branches are never deleted: closing the draft PR would lose the work a human retry reuses, so a human or a janitor removes them.\n4. **`:running` with no record.** Every 5 minutes, for each configured triage, worker and reviewer role, an open issue or PR that has the `:running` label but no running job (and none that ended in the last 10 minutes) gets one comment, `ard:executor:failed` and the label removed. It is a core-API list per role, not a search. It is skipped in dry-run, whose database never matches real labels, and for issues held with `ard:hold`.\n\nHost log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused.\n\nSuccessful jobs clean up at close-out: the worktree is removed, the Kubernetes `Job` gets a short TTL, and logs stay 7 days.\n\nHost job steps run with `GOFLAGS=-modcacherw`, so a Go module cache downloaded into the job home is writable. The module cache is per job and is not shared, because a cache that jobs can write to would let one job poison modules that later jobs build. Cleanup restores owner write permission on the whole job home and worktree before removing them, and never follows symlinks, so read-only trees no longer leak.\n\n## Guardrails\n\nAgents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n\n- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branc\n- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) \n- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.\n- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake la\n- **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's own cost block.\n- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, \n- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent \n- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/\n- **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API) are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a write-access human approval. The App bypasses `main`'s ruleset (its direct `.ard/` commits need it), so the m\n- **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, \n- **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as \n- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit\n- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` sin\n- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in th\n- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the j\n## Open questions\n\nAll Phase 0 decisions (#1 to #9) are recorded above.\n\n- [x] **Headless modes for `kimi` and `dsh-tui`.** ([#7](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/7)) Decided: support `kimi`, defer `dsh-tui`. `kimi -p \"<prompt>\" --output-format stream-json` runs headless with stdin closed. It has no permission m\n7:ard is a controller that automates repository development: scanners → triage → worker → reviewers → merger, coordinated through `ard:` GitHub labels. The design is [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md). Read the relevant section before changing behaviour.\n9:**docs/proposal.md is the source of truth for design.** A PR that changes behaviour updates the proposal in the same PR. If code and proposal disagree, raise it on the issue rather than silently picking one.\n26:- The language choice is tracked as a decision issue; if it changes, update this file.\n60:- Decision issues (`type:decision`) record the choice in a comment, then update the proposal and close.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b148c304-e519-4583-b0a3-cdc55e43f269","uuid":"c7796b13-b566-4362-87bd-9b6f7c08ec40","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:08:29.767Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the co\n\n|  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | `git worktree` of the controller's clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`) under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/<job-id>`; sidecar exported into the job home | `Job` with an init container that shallow-clones the branch into an `emptyDir`; sidecar cloned by an init container (Phase 3) |\n| Runtime | the role's `Dockerfile` image under Docker if it has one, else a host subprocess; hard timeout | a pod from the role's `Dockerfile` image, or the default `ard-agent` image; hard timeout |\n| Heartbeat | PID alive plus log file mtime | pod phase plus container restarts |\n| Logs | streamed to `~/.ard/logs/<owner>/<repo>/<issue#>/` while running; committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out | pod logs, committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/` at close-out |\n| Labels on resources | job id in the directory name | `ard/issue`, `ard/stage`, `ard/role`, `ard/job` |\n| Good for | Phase 1, one host, fast iteration | parallelism, isolation, untrusted inputs |\n\n**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ar\n\n**The agent's contract is narrow.** It may edit files in its worktree and must write `.ard/<issue#>/<stage>-<role>.md` with a `status`. Under `.ard/<issue#>/` it may change only its own role file and what its role's allowlist grants (see the `.ard/` contract). Exit 0 without that file counts as a failure, not a success, and so does a malformed file. The file counts only if this job wrote it: before the agent starts, the executor removes the job's role file from the worktree (`executor.Job.Scrub`), so a copy left from an earlier stage or run is never trusted. A role file the agent never wrote again fails the job (`role file not written by this job`, or `role file missing` when none was ever there). One it wrote again with identical bytes still counts, but only because the executor recorded the scrub in the handle (`Handle.Scrubbed`); close-out refuses an unscrubbed, unchanged role file (`\n\n**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop a\n\n**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-p\n\n**The Reaper runs every minute and decides failure, not the agent.** It marks a job failed when the process or pod is gone without a close-out, the deadline passes (it kills the job first), the heartbeat goes quiet for 10 minutes, or the job is `:running` with no record in local state. Every failure goes through close-out, so it is resumable and never double-comments:\n\n1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\n2. **Close-out.